<h3 id="4-be-cautious-with-indexing" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight"><span>4. Be Cautious with Indexing</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#4-be-cautious-with-indexing" aria-label="Link to 4. Be Cautious with Indexing"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>Regarding indexing the <code>deleted_at</code> column, there&#x27;s debate in the community. Some argue against it because:</p>
<ul>
<li>Most queries filter for non-deleted records (<code>WHERE deleted_at IS NULL</code>), which may not benefit from an index on <code>deleted_at</code>.</li>
<li>An index on <code>deleted_at</code> could potentially slow down write operations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Instead, consider your specific use case:</p>
<ul>
<li>If you frequently query for soft-deleted records or restore them, an index might be beneficial.</li>
<li>If your primary operations are on non-deleted records, you might not need an index on <code>deleted_at</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Always measure the performance impact in your specific scenario before deciding on indexing strategy.</p>
<p>For more information about indexes in general, refer to Neon&#x27;s documentation on <a class="" href="/docs/postgresql/index-types">indexes</a>.</p>
